"Bose–Einstein Condensation | VirtualDemos",
"Bose–Einstein Condensation",
"Student laboratory · Modern Physics and Thermodynamics",
"Textbook: OpenStax <em>University Physics</em>, Vol. {#}, §{#} (matter waves), §{#} (the particle in a box) and §{#} (the harmonic oscillator), and Vol. {#}, §{#} (the distribution of molecular speeds). Bose–Einstein condensation itself goes beyond the textbook; the Background gives what you need. Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#}.",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Cool a hot gas to a condensate; Where the packets touch; Almost all condensed; A lighter atom: sodium; Few atoms, big box; Warm a condensate back up; The velocity picture of {#}; In a harmonic trap, like the real experiments; The {#} picture, from a trap.",
"The gas is \\(N\\) atoms that do not interact, in two dimensions, held either in a square box of side \\(L\\) with hard walls or in a round harmonic trap of frequency \\(f\\). Choose the atom (rubidium {#}, sodium {#}, lithium {#}, hydrogen or helium {#}), the number of atoms ({#} to {#}), the box side ({#} to {#} µm) or the trap frequency ({#} to {#} Hz), and the temperature ({#} nK to {#} µK). The readouts beside the sliders also take typed values.",
"The demo works out exactly how many atoms, on average, sit in each energy level at that temperature (the Bose–Einstein distribution). The readouts give the thermal wavelength, the overlap of the packets, the temperature at which they touch (box) or the critical temperature (trap), and the number of atoms in the ground state \\(N_0\\).",
"The temperature only changes while the demo plays with Cool down or Warm up pressed; otherwise it stays where you set it.",
"The thermal atoms are drawn as wave packets of the thermal wavelength; the ground state is drawn exactly. The ladder on the left shows one sample of which level each atom is in.",
"Record reading stores the container, the atom, the settings, the temperature and the readouts. Box readings go to Table {#} and trap readings to Table {#}, so clear the readings before you switch containers. Export to worksheet sends the readings to the worksheet; Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of the table.",
"Blind (Lab section) hides the ground state count, so you can predict it first. Readings cannot be recorded while it is on.",
"Measure how the de Broglie wavelength of the atoms in a gas grows as it cools, find the temperature at which the atoms’ waves overlap, and watch the atoms pile into a single quantum state: a Bose–Einstein condensate. Then compare a box with a harmonic trap, the way real condensates are made.",
"Every atom is also a wave (§{#}). In a gas at temperature \\(T\\) the size of an atom’s wave packet is set by its thermal de Broglie wavelength,",
"\\[\\lambda_T = \\frac{h}{\\sqrt{2\\pi m k_B T}},\\]",
"which grows as the gas cools. In a hot gas \\(\\lambda_T\\) is far smaller than the distance between atoms, and the atoms behave like little billiard balls. In two dimensions \\(N\\) atoms in an area \\(L^2\\) are about \\(L/\\sqrt{N}\\) apart, so their packets touch when \\(N\\lambda_T^2/L^2 = 1\\), which happens at",
"\\[T_{\\text{touch}} = \\frac{h^2}{2\\pi m k_B}\\,\\frac{N}{L^2}.\\]",
"Atoms like rubidium {#} are bosons: any number of them can share one quantum state. On average a level of energy \\(E\\) holds",
"\\[\\langle n \\rangle = \\frac{1}{e^{(E - \\mu)/k_B T} - 1}\\]",
"atoms, where the chemical potential \\(\\mu\\) is whatever makes the levels add up to \\(N\\) atoms. Once the packets overlap, the excited levels cannot hold all the atoms, and the rest crowd into the ground state. That crowd, all described by one wavefunction, is the condensate.",
"In the box the levels are those of a particle in a two dimensional box (§{#}), \\(E = \\frac{h^2}{8mL^2}(n_x^2 + n_y^2)\\). In a round harmonic trap they are evenly spaced (§{#}), \\(E = \\hbar\\omega(n_x + n_y + 1)\\) with \\(\\omega = 2\\pi f\\). For many atoms in such a trap the condensate appears below a critical temperature, and below it the share of atoms in the ground state is",
"\\[k_B T_c = \\frac{\\hbar\\omega\\sqrt{6N}}{\\pi}, \\qquad \\frac{N_0}{N} = 1 - \\left(\\frac{T}{T_c}\\right)^2 .\\]",
"A trap with only a few hundred atoms follows this only roughly; part of this lab is to see how closely.",
